Provenance and licence of the Richards program
==============================================

Richards is Martin Richards's operating-system scheduler simulation benchmark. These files are
ports of the JavaScript version in Are We Fast Yet (Stefan Marr, Benoit Daloze and Hanspeter
Mössenböck, https://github.com/smarr/are-we-fast-yet at commit 74306fec,
benchmarks/JavaScript/richards.js), which says of itself:

    The benchmark in its current state is a derivation from the SOM version,
    which is derived from Mario Wolczko's Smalltalk version of DeltaBlue.

    The original license details are availble here:
    http://web.archive.org/web/20050825101121/http://www.sunlabs.com/people/mario/java_benchmarking/index.html

("of DeltaBlue" is AWFY's wording in richards.js; the file is Richards.) Its LICENSE.md says,
for Richards and DeltaBlue:

    These benchmark are derived from the Smalltalk sources provided by Mario Wolczko.
    License details are available at:
      http://web.archive.org/web/20050825101121/http://www.sunlabs.com/people/mario/java_benchmarking/index.html
    Further information:
      http://www.wolczko.com/java_benchmarking.html

The harness code AWFY's richards.js extends (benchmark.js) is "Copyright (c) 2015-2016 Stefan
Marr" under the MIT licence; none of it is copied here. The archived page's licensing terms
were not retrieved for this commit. A maintainer must confirm those terms before this program
is redistributed outside this repository, as for bench/programs/deltablue.

Changes from the Are We Fast Yet version
----------------------------------------

js/main.mjs keeps AWFY's classes, methods and closures, without the Benchmark class. All
versions:

- return a count instead of a boolean: run(n) runs benchmark() n times and returns the
  number of runs that end with AWFY's expected counts (23246 queued packets and 9297 holds),
  so a correct run(n) is n;
- leave out the trace code, which AWFY disables (TRACING = false); js/main.mjs keeps it.

sakura/main.sak reimplements the same scheduler over two persistent lists, task control
blocks indexed by identity (AWFY's taskTable) and packets indexed by creation order, threaded
through every scheduler operation, because Sakura Lang has no mutable objects; every
reference is an index and -1 is NO_TASK / NO_WORK. The Sakura and native ports (C, Rust, Go,
OCaml, Koka) dispatch on the task's data-record variant where AWFY calls a closure created
with that record. Sakura Lang has no bitwise operators, so the idler's `control & 1` and
`(control / 2) ^ 53256` are a remainder and a digit-by-digit XOR there. The C, Go and OCaml
ports link mutable objects by pointer; the Rust port keeps them in arenas indexed by
identity and creation order; the Koka port threads vectors of records as the Sakura program
does. Every traversal, queue and update keeps AWFY's order.
